      "id": 1,
      "text": "Cottonwood is an unincorporated community in Sequoyah County, located near the Arkansas state border and the Arkansas River valley.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        18,
        22
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://grokipedia.com/page/cottonwood_sequoyah_county_oklahoma"
    },
    {
      "id": 2,
      "text": "The terrain surrounding Cottonwood consists of low-lying river bottoms, bayous, and sloughs formed by the influence of the Arkansas River.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        3
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://grokipedia.com/page/cottonwood_sequoyah_county_oklahoma"
    },
    {
      "id": 3,
      "text": "Cottonwood emerged after the Civil War as a settlement primarily inhabited by non-citizen settlers in Cherokee Nation territory.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        4
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://grokipedia.com/page/cottonwood_sequoyah_county_oklahoma"
    },
    {
      "id": 4,
      "text": "The arrival of the Kansas and Arkansas Valley Railroad in 1890 helped foster the development of the Cottonwood community.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        7
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://grokipedia.com/page/cottonwood_sequoyah_county_oklahoma"
    },
    {
      "id": 5,
      "text": "Cotton emerged as the dominant cash crop in Cottonwood, supporting economic growth through the 1910s and 1920s.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        8
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://grokipedia.com/page/cottonwood_sequoyah_county_oklahoma"
    },
    {
      "id": 6,
      "text": "Cottonwood experiences a humid subtropical climate with hot, humid summers and mild winters typical of eastern Oklahoma.",
